{"context":"The CLI is the primary interface to Anvil. Every user interaction — from initial project setup to production deployment — flows through the command line. The quality of the CLI design directly determines the quality of the user experience.\n\nWhen CLI design is treated as an implementation detail rather than a product concern, the following problems emerge:\n\n**Implementation-oriented commands.** Commands reflect the internal source code structure rather than the user's mental model. Users must understand how Anvil is implemented to know which command to run. A change in internal structure requires changing command names, breaking user workflows.\n\n**Inconsistent patterns.** Each command follows different conventions for options, arguments, output, and error reporting. Users must learn each co Source: ADR-010-cli-experience-and-command-design.md Date:2026-07-19 Status:Accepted.","decision":"Anvil adopts a user-centric command architecture.\n\nThe CLI is designed around user intent and product concepts, not around internal implementation.\n\n- Commands expose product capabilities — what the user can DO with Anvil\n- Commands do not expose internal implementation — how Anvil implements those capabilities\n- The CLI reflects the product domain (releases, artifacts, pipelines, configuration) rather than the source code structure (packages, modules, classes, functions)\n\nThe CLI is the product interface. It is a first-class architectural concern, not a thin wrapper around internal APIs.","consequences":"### 11.1 Positive Consequences\n\n**Better developer experience.** Users interact with product concepts they understand, not implementation details. The CLI is approachable for new users and efficient for experienced users.\n\n**Lower learning curve.** Consistent patterns mean that learning one command transfers to all commands. Users spend less time reading documentation and more time accomplishing tasks.\n\n**Stable automation.** Deterministic exit codes, stable output formats, and non-interactive defaults mean that automation scripts work reliably across versions.\n\n**Consistent command model.** All commands follow the same conventions. There are no special cases, no irregular patterns, and no hidden behaviors.\n\n**Future extensibility.** New capabilities map to new command groups following est","alternativesConsidered":"Considered\n\n### 12.1 Implementation-Oriented Commands\n\n**Description:** Commands are organized around internal subsystems — `engine`, `service`, `runner`, `manager`. Users must understand the internal architecture to use the CLI effectively.\n\n**Rejected because:** This approach couples the CLI to the implementation. A change in internal structure requires changing command names, breaking user workflows. Users should not need to understand how Anvil is built to use it effectively.\n\n### 12.2 Frame"}